miadi-terminal — clickable miadi-chronicle references on a Miadi client

A bare miadi-chronicle:126 in a terminal opens that episode in the browser.
A ceremony or circle opens its page the same way: miadi-circle:<id>,
miadi-ceremony:<id>, or the bare wheel id, circle:1790787727155:2slscw.
A foundation packet opens its page too: miadi-foundation:<slug>[/<file>].

  sudo apt install miadi-terminal
  miadi-terminal front https://<your Miadi>   # only when MIADI_URL_BASE is not already it
  miadi-terminal enable                       # once per user: every integration present
  miadi-terminal status                       # what a click does here

Integrations, each enabled per user (`miadi-terminal enable tmux`, …):

  desktop     the miadi-chronicle:, miadi-ceremony:, miadi-circle: and miadi-foundation: schemes
              open in the browser from any application: OSC 8 links in a
              terminal, links in a web page.
              /usr/share/applications/miadi-chronicle-open.desktop
  terminator  Ctrl+click a bare reference in Terminator's output.
              /usr/share/miadi-terminal/terminator/, linked into Terminator's
              plugin directory; enabling adds it to enabled_plugins.
  tmux        click a bare reference in a tmux pane (tmux 3.2+, mouse on).
              /usr/share/miadi-terminal/tmux/miadi-chronicle.conf; enabling
              adds a source-file line to your tmux config.
  defaults    every tmux on the machine reads /etc/tmux.conf before the
              user's ~/.tmux.conf, and the one installed here reads
              /etc/miadi/tmux.conf: the mouse on, wheel scrolling, 50,000
              lines of history, 24-bit colour, extended keys and focus
              events. A user's own line wins. Enabling comments out the
              lines of the user's config that repeat one there, marked
              "# in /etc/miadi/tmux.conf: "; disable puts them back. Both
              files are conffiles: dpkg keeps an edited copy, and asks
              once when a host already had its own /etc/tmux.conf.
  restore     tmux sessions that survive a reboot or a crash. Enabled only by
              name: miadi-terminal enable restore. It clones tpm,
              tmux-resurrect and tmux-continuum into ~/.tmux/plugins, adds a
              source-file line for
              /usr/share/miadi-terminal/session-continuity/session-continuity.conf,
              and enables two user units from /usr/lib/systemd/user:
              tmux-server.service starts the tmux server at boot (with linger)
              once the desktop login's environment is there, so continuum
              restores the last save and tide (miadi-tide) relaunches each
              agent with its session. tmux-save.timer saves every 15 minutes,
              attached client or not. Keep one tmux version on the machine: a
              client of another version cannot attach ("open terminal failed:
              not a terminal").

enable changes only that user's configs: Terminator's enabled_plugins, lines
in the tmux config, the scheme default in ~/.config/mimeapps.list. Each
keeps a .bak-miadi-terminal copy of how it was before the first change. The
plugin and wrapper an earlier `inquiry-weave terminal install` left are moved
to ~/.local/share/miadi-terminal/legacy/. Without Terminator installed, the
plugin link still creates Terminator's plugin directory; nothing loads it.

Nothing is resolved on this machine. /usr/bin/miadi-chronicle-open turns the
reference into <front>/api/chronicle/open?uri=<reference>, and the Miadi server
redirects to the page. The front is MIADI_CHRONICLE_OPEN_URL, else
MIADI_URL_BASE, read through miadi-config.

For another user on the machine, an administrator runs it as that user:

  sudo -iu <user> miadi-terminal enable
  sudo -iu <user> env XDG_RUNTIME_DIR=/run/user/$(id -u <user>) miadi-terminal enable restore

restore needs XDG_RUNTIME_DIR to reach that user's systemd, which runs once
linger is on (sudo loginctl enable-linger <user>). The other steps for a new
user are in /usr/share/doc/miadi/README.

Source: https://github.com/jgwill/miadi-orchestration-kit, packages/miadi/deb
Restore: the same layer jgwill/gaia linux_migration/14-tmux-resurrect.sh
         installs on gaia (jgwill/gaia#90). tests/session-continuity-sync.sh
         in packages/miadi/deb checks that the copies match.
Spec:   https://github.com/jgwill/Miadi, rispecs/miadi-chronicle-dsl/SPEC-TERMINAL.md
